#Problem APEX 6.1.30

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Techniques of integration)
## DBsection(Substitution (without trigonometric functions))
## KEYWORDS('indefinite integral')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('6.1')
## Problem1('30')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',      'PGML.pl',
    'contextFraction.pl', 'parserFormulaUpToConstant.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Numeric")->flags->set(reduceConstantFunctions => 0);

## (a) ##
$a1 = random(2, 9);
$b1 = random(2, 9) * random(-1, 1, 2);

$u1 = "$b1 x";
$f1 = Formula("$a1^{$u1}");

#### Answers ####

$F1 = FormulaUpToConstant("$a1^{$u1} / ($b1 ln($a1))");

#################

BEGIN_PGML
Evaluate the indefinite integral using substitution. (Use [`C`]
for the constant of integration.)

[`  \int [$f1] \, dx = `] [_]{$F1}{40}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Substitute [`u = [$u1]`].  Then [`du = [$b1] \,dx`].

[```
  \begin{aligned}
      \int [$f1] \, dx & = 
     \dfrac{1}{[$b1]} \int [$a1]^u \, du \\
    & =  \dfrac{1}{[$b1]} \cdot \dfrac{[$a1]^u}{\ln [$a1]} + C\\
    & =  \dfrac{[$a1]^{[$u1]}}{[$b1] \ln [$a1]} + C. \\
  \end{aligned}
```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
